<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augsburg Confession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Confessio Augustana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> Confession is the founding statement of Lutheran belief, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> and presented to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the Diet of Augsburg in 1530. It was signed by several princes, including Wolfgang of Anhalt, and by the imperial cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and Reutlingen, and other cities soon subscribed. It became the legal basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession.jpg" alt="Augsburg Confession" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburger-Reichstag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and Justus Jonas had drafted a statement of their views in the Articles of Schwabach in 1529. On 21 January 1530 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> to meet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, to restore religious and political unity and rally support against the Ottoman advance after the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Some Protestants received the invitation with suspicion. Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-saxony">John of Saxony</a> asked Luther, Jonas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a> and Melanchthon to prepare a summary of the Lutheran faith, which became known as the Torgau Articles.</p><p>Luther was left at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> because he was an outlaw under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a>. Melanchthon worked on a larger draft during the journey and sent it to Luther on 11 May, who approved it. The final text was adopted on 23 June. It was read in German by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> chancellor Christian Beyer on 25 June 1530 in the small chapel of the episcopal palace, a venue chosen to exclude the public, and the reading lasted two hours. Neither the German nor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> copy handed to the emperor survives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Confession has twenty-eight articles, set out in positive statements and in negative ones. The first twenty-one describe the principles of Christian faith held by the Lutherans, and the last seven describe what they regarded as abuses in the Roman Catholic church. The signers included Elector John of Saxony, Margrave George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, Duke Ernest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, Landgrave Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and Prince Wolfgang of Anhalt, together with representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and Reutlingen. The cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenburg-in-bayern">Weißenburg in Bayern</a>, Heilbronn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> and Windesheim also declared their concurrence. It states that in doctrine and ceremonies nothing had been received against Scripture or the Church Catholic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Twenty Catholic theologians approved nine articles, approved six with qualifications and condemned thirteen. Their reply, the Confutation, was poorly prepared and never widely published. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a> answered it in the Apology of the Augsburg Confession. The Lutheran princes formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> of 1546 to 1547 was won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> without settling the religious question. Eight years later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> legal status in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>In 1540 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a> produced a revised edition, the Variata, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>; many Lutheran churches subscribe instead to the Unaltered Augsburg Confession. The Confession and the Apology are foundational documents of the Book of Concord of 1580. An English translation of 1536 influenced the Anglican articles of faith, culminating in the Thirty-Nine Articles of 1563. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a>'s Reformation Symphony marked its 300th anniversary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augsburg Confession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1530 the Confutatio Augustana answered the Lutheran <b>Augsburg Confession</b>, and in 1540 the Jesuits were canonically recognised.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>New texts gave the churches their shape: Luther’s German Bible and his Small Catechism of 1529, the <b>Augsburg Confession</b> drawn up by Philip Melanchthon in 1530, Thomas Cranmer’s Book of Common Prayer of 1549 and Reformed statements of faith such as the Heidelberg Catechism of 1563.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Diet of Augsburg, German rulers and free cities presented Emperor Charles V with the <b>Augsburg Confession</b>, prepared by Philipp Melanchthon at the behest of John, Elector of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-25">25 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Augsburg Confession</b> was presented at the diet of 1530, and the Peace of Augsburg of 1555 recognised Lutheranism.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Free Imperial City of Augsburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the preacher Matthäus Alber it adopted the Reformation in the 1520s, and in 1530 it was one of only two cities to sign the <b>Augsburg Confession</b>, with Nuremberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-reutlingen">Free Imperial City of Reutlingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Windsheim adopted the Reformation in the 1520s and subscribed to the <b>Augsburg Confession</b> soon after it was presented in 1530.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-windsheim">Free Imperial City of Windsheim</a></li></ul></section>
